diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-layer investigation of the simple_bgp topology found no fault matching the user's reported one-way communication problem. Phase 1 reachability testing showed 0% packet loss in both directions — pc1 (195.11.14.2) → pc2 (200.1.1.2) and pc2 → pc1 — confirming that the IP data-plane is fully bidirectional at the time of investigation. The L2 sweep (l2_snapshot) found no duplicate MAC addresses and no down or missing interfaces. The infrastructure sweep (infra_sweep) found no nftables or iptables ACL rules anywhere in the topology that could selectively block TCP return traffic, no static ARP poisoning, and no addressing or gateway divergence between hosts. The BGP control plane is healthy: both router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) have their BGP sessions in Established state, router1 correctly advertises 195.11.14.0/24 and has installed a return route to 200.1.1.0/24 via router2, and router2 correctly advertises 200.1.1.0/24 and has installed a return route to 195.11.14.0/24 via router1 — the path is fully symmetric. The TC snapshot found no bandwidth-limiting, packet-corruption, or qdisc shaping on any interface. The pressure sweep found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion. There is no mechanism present that could cause the reported asymmetric TCP failure; the claim is unconfirmed by any live evidence. No corrective action is required on the network side.
=== END ===